## Proof

**Answer: Yes.** There exists an infinite set whose cardinality is not equal to that of any member of $W$.

---

**Construction.** Define the iterated power-set operation on $\mathbb{N}$: let $P^0(\mathbb{N}) = \mathbb{N}$ and $P^{k+1}(\mathbb{N}) = P(P^k(\mathbb{N}))$ for $k \in \mathbb{N}$. The class function $k \mapsto P^k(\mathbb{N})$ is definable in ZFC, so by the **Axiom of Replacement** (applied to the set $\mathbb{N}$), its image
$$W = \{P^k(\mathbb{N}) : k \in \mathbb{N}\}$$
is a set. By the **Axiom of Union**, the set
$$U \;=\; \bigcup W \;=\; \bigcup_{k \in \mathbb{N}} P^k(\mathbb{N})$$
exists. We claim $U$ is the desired infinite set.

---

**$U$ is infinite.** Since $\mathbb{N} = P^0(\mathbb{N}) \in W$, we have $\mathbb{N} \subseteq U$, so $U$ is infinite.

---

**$|U| \neq |P^k(\mathbb{N})|$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$.**

Fix any $k \in \mathbb{N}$. Since $P^k(\mathbb{N}) \in W$, every element of $P^k(\mathbb{N})$ is an element of some member of $W$, hence $P^k(\mathbb{N}) \subseteq U$. Therefore
$$|P^k(\mathbb{N})| \;\le\; |U|. \tag{1}$$

By **Cantor's theorem**, applied to the set $P^k(\mathbb{N})$:
$$|P^k(\mathbb{N})| \;<\; |P^{k+1}(\mathbb{N})|. \tag{2}$$

Since $P^{k+1}(\mathbb{N}) \in W$, the same argument gives $P^{k+1}(\mathbb{N}) \subseteq U$, hence
$$|P^{k+1}(\mathbb{N})| \;\le\; |U|. \tag{3}$$

Combining (2) and (3):
$$|P^k(\mathbb{N})| \;<\; |P^{k+1}(\mathbb{N})| \;\le\; |U|,$$
so $|P^k(\mathbb{N})| < |U|$, which in particular gives $|P^k(\mathbb{N})| \neq |U|$.

Since $k$ was arbitrary, $|U|$ is not equal to $|P^k(\mathbb{N})|$ for any $k \in \mathbb{N}$, i.e., $|U|$ is not the cardinality of any member of $W$.

---

**Conclusion.** The set $U = \bigcup_{k \in \mathbb{N}} P^k(\mathbb{N})$ is infinite, and its cardinality is strictly larger than $|P^k(\mathbb{N})|$ for every $k$, hence differs from the cardinality of every member of $W$. $\blacksquare$

